WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:16.470
Hello! Welcome to Superstars in History.

2
00:00:16.971 --> 00:00:18.932
I'm your host, Faye Moss.

3
00:00:19.812 --> 00:00:25.175
Today we have another exclusive interview with a famous person from the past.

4
00:00:26.055 --> 00:00:31.258
This guest was a playwright who thrilled audiences with his gripping stories.

5
00:00:31.799 --> 00:00:38.454
His works are still super popular, and some people regard him as the greatest writer ever.

6
00:00:39.434 --> 00:00:44.316
Coming to us straight from history, here's William Shakespeare!

7
00:01:02.003 --> 00:01:04.796
How fare you, fair Faye?

8
00:01:04.816 --> 00:01:08.679
All the world's a stage, but how good it is to be onstage with you!

9
00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.761
Well, hello to you too!

10
00:01:12.902 --> 00:01:17.185
Every story needs a good beginning, so why don't we start with yours?

11
00:01:18.286 --> 00:01:22.689
I was born in 1564 in the village of Stratford-upon-Avon in England.

12
00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.012
I was the first surviving child in my family.

13
00:01:26.993 --> 00:01:31.376
My mother was the daughter of a wealthy farming family, and my father was a glove maker.

14
00:01:32.288 --> 00:01:36.050
He was active in local politics, serving in a variety of posts.

15
00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:42.553
He provided well for our family but eventually found himself in trouble for conducting unlawful business.

16
00:01:43.994 --> 00:01:44.774
Oh my.

17
00:01:45.355 --> 00:01:48.276
But he must have been able to pay for a good education.

18
00:01:49.256 --> 00:01:52.618
After all, you are known as a master of the English language.

19
00:01:54.419 --> 00:01:56.080
Was that your favorite subject?

20
00:01:57.088 --> 00:02:02.271
The nearby grammar school required no tuition fee, dear Faye, so there was no expense for my family.

21
00:02:03.171 --> 00:02:05.853
As for subjects, pupils did not study English.

22
00:02:06.833 --> 00:02:10.115
Schools in my day provided instruction in the great language of Latin.

23
00:02:10.956 --> 00:02:15.558
The school curriculum included translating and performing Roman dramas as well.

24
00:02:16.479 --> 00:02:21.942
Dramas? I guess your schooling gave you a head start on becoming a playwright then!

25
00:02:23.022 --> 00:02:25.724
I did not become a playwright until later in life, Faye.

26
00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:31.923
In 1582, at the tender age of 18, I married a woman named Anne Hathaway.

27
00:02:32.843 --> 00:02:39.386
The next year our daughter Susanna was born, and in 1585 our twins, Judith and Hamnet, were born.

28
00:02:40.347 --> 00:02:45.589
My family stayed behind in Stratford when I eventually made my way to London and began work as

29
00:02:45.629 --> 00:02:46.990
an actor and playwright.

30
00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:51.672
In those days theaters in London were pretty new, right?

31
00:02:53.076 --> 00:02:59.118
Aye, under the reign of Her Majesty Queen Elizabeth, several playhouses were built in London, and nonreligious plays

32
00:02:59.158 --> 00:03:01.058
became a beloved form of amusement.

33
00:03:02.099 --> 00:03:03.419
It was an exciting time!

34
00:03:04.379 --> 00:03:08.841
I had the fortune to become part owner in a theater company, the Lord Chamberlain's Men.

35
00:03:09.781 --> 00:03:14.462
We were widely celebrated, and we gave several performances at Her Majesty's court.

36
00:03:15.383 --> 00:03:19.684
We even built our own theater, the Globe, on the south bank of the River Thames.

37
00:03:20.688 --> 00:03:23.489
Up to three thousand people could watch our performances.

38
00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:30.712
Performances back then were pretty different compared to today—and not very quiet.

39
00:03:31.993 --> 00:03:36.354
Audiences often cheered good guys and heckled bad guys.

40
00:03:37.555 --> 00:03:40.856
Oh! And there wasn't much scenery or use of props.

41
00:03:41.937 --> 00:03:43.997
Words were my scenery, my lady.

42
00:03:44.958 --> 00:03:50.180
If I wanted the audience to visualize the play's setting, I crafted dialogue to paint that picture.

43
00:03:51.008 --> 00:03:57.470
For example, in King Lear, I made a character say "such sheets of fire, such bursts of horrid

44
00:03:57.530 --> 00:03:59.931
thunder" to tell the audience there was a storm.

45
00:04:01.051 --> 00:04:06.272
We had a few props onstage too, including a cannon that caused a slight problem when it burned

46
00:04:06.312 --> 00:04:08.813
the theater down during a performance of Henry VIII.

47
00:04:10.334 --> 00:04:15.195
Yikes! Today your most popular play is probably Hamlet.

48
00:04:16.115 --> 00:04:18.876
It's a great play, but a bit depressing.

49
00:04:20.712 --> 00:04:22.973
Almost everyone dies at the end.

50
00:04:23.854 --> 00:04:28.096
Hamlet is a tragedy, which by definition, does not end well!

51
00:04:30.058 --> 00:04:32.859
And it is a ghost story and a revenge story.

52
00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.881
But my tragedies aren't completely tragic.

53
00:04:36.781 --> 00:04:38.743
I like to include a touch of humor too.

54
00:04:39.803 --> 00:04:43.966
In Macbeth, a drunken gatekeeper makes a funny appearance between serious scenes.

55
00:04:44.926 --> 00:04:47.648
The gatekeeper's appearance originally had a practical purpose.

56
00:04:48.512 --> 00:04:52.816
It gave the actors who played Macbeth and Lady Macbeth time to change clothes between scenes.

57
00:04:53.836 --> 00:04:58.780
Wow. I'm getting behind-the-scenes info from Shakespeare himself!

58
00:05:00.781 --> 00:05:02.483
I'm curious about something else.

59
00:05:04.344 --> 00:05:13.311
I hope this won't offend you, but despite your great reputation, you're known for um taking ideas from

60
00:05:13.411 --> 00:05:14.592
books and other plays.

61
00:05:15.804 --> 00:05:19.727
Very few of my plays were completely original, wise Faye.

62
00:05:20.688 --> 00:05:25.812
I borrowed characters and plots from the mythology of Rome and Greece and was well read on the

63
00:05:25.852 --> 00:05:27.153
history of the British Isles.

64
00:05:28.154 --> 00:05:32.598
I was also familiar with popular works from the Continent and paid close attention to the tastes of

65
00:05:32.618 --> 00:05:33.018
the day.

66
00:05:33.939 --> 00:05:38.603
Competition on the London theatrical scene was quite fierce, but my company thrived.

67
00:05:39.704 --> 00:05:43.267
You even performed for many powerful people in your day.

68
00:05:43.727 --> 00:05:44.768
What was that like?

69
00:05:45.756 --> 00:05:47.597
At times it was inconvenient!

70
00:05:48.577 --> 00:05:52.998
One night we were hired to give a performance of The Comedy of Errors for a group of

71
00:05:53.098 --> 00:05:54.238
powerful lawyers.

72
00:05:55.159 --> 00:06:00.200
But on the same evening, Her Majesty had commanded a performance at court, and we couldn't tell our

73
00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:02.501
gracious queen that we already had plans!

74
00:06:03.501 --> 00:06:09.103
After performing for the queen, we arrived very late to our second engagement, creating much ado!

75
00:06:10.103 --> 00:06:13.224
It was nearly midnight, and by then the stage had been dismantled.

76
00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:17.501
The entire affair became called "The Night of Errors."

77
00:06:18.602 --> 00:06:22.303
Queen Elizabeth wasn't the only monarch you performed for.

78
00:06:23.344 --> 00:06:26.365
Later your company had a royal sponsor.

79
00:06:27.345 --> 00:06:33.068
Yes. When Queen Elizabeth died, King James of Scotland ascended the throne of England, and he became the

80
00:06:33.128 --> 00:06:36.429
patron of my company, which became known as the King's Men.

81
00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:39.850
We performed at court even more often.

82
00:06:40.771 --> 00:06:43.452
In fact, His Majesty greatly influenced Macbeth.

83
00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:49.224
I was inspired to include evil witches as characters in the play because the king had a great

84
00:06:49.284 --> 00:06:53.207
interest in the supernatural and had even written his own book on the subject.

85
00:06:54.168 --> 00:06:57.831
I also included details that would flatter His Majesty's ancestors.

86
00:06:58.932 --> 00:07:01.995
You certainly did have a dramatic career.

87
00:07:03.035 --> 00:07:05.397
Thank you for sharing your story with us today.

88
00:07:06.318 --> 00:07:08.500
We'll let you get back to history now.

89
00:07:09.481 --> 00:07:11.562
Parting is such sweet sorrow.

90
00:07:11.943 --> 00:07:13.184
Good day to you, my lady.

91
00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:24.895
Over his career Shakespeare wrote a large body of work—at least 39 plays and hundreds of poems, including

92
00:07:24.915 --> 00:07:25.196
154 sonnets.

93
00:07:25.236 --> 00:07:26.837
He died in April of 1616 at the age of 52.

94
00:07:27.917 --> 00:07:29.579
His incredible influence still endures too.

95
00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:46.125
Many common words in the English language, such as "fashionable," "gossip," and "puppy dog," were first written down

96
00:07:46.305 --> 00:07:47.486
by Shakespeare!

97
00:07:48.446 --> 00:07:56.731
And his plays have been translated into hundreds of languages, reaching readers and audiences all around the world.

98
00:07:57.872 --> 00:07:59.713
I hope you enjoyed today's interview.

99
00:08:00.634 --> 00:08:03.936
Until next time, I bid you farewell!
